WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.440
On today's AI briefing, we think a lean proof that compiles under an outside check is the most checkable form this claim could take.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.860
This is the AI briefing for Saturday, November 21.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.000
I'm Michael.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:23.400
Like everything here, this briefing is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.280
Two proofs of the Riemann hypothesis surfaced in the same week.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:35.380
One was written with help from Anthropics clawed in the proof language lien, and an MIT mathematician has confirmed that it compiles.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:42.280
The other is a 940-page draft written entirely by a human, Alessandro Masalo.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:52.300
OpenAI says its coding agents now finish multi-hour tasks with no human help 46% of the time, up from 23% in March.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.860
Raymez Nam argues that the longer horizons still trail the forecasts.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:03.040
We also have an Ottawa Defense startup's $50 million uncrewed combat aircraft project

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.660
and a thinking machine's model that reruns its own layers to reason harder.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.500
The main stories come first, then a few quick hits.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.900
First, mathematics and one of its oldest open problems.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:24.240
This weak tech crunch and latent space both covered two separate claims to have proved the Riemann hypothesis.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.600
The first comes from Levin Alperge at Anthropic.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:34.980
He says he produced a proof with help from Claude, written in Lean, the formal proof language, so that a machine can check it.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:41.240
The proof is enormous. 1.3 gigabytes, or about 250k pages.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.000
Andrew Sutherland at MIT has independently confirmed that it compiles.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.000
The second claim is entirely human. Stanford's Ravi Vackel announced a proof by Alessandro Masullo

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.820
of the generalized Riemann hypothesis, a 940-page draft. Brian Conrad has reviewed it,

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:07.680
but it is not yet verified as a proof of RH. So how solid is this? Compilation has been confirmed

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:15.240
from outside Anthropic. Beyond that, neither proof has been verified. Lean accepting the code does

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:20.740
not show that the theorem it states really is the Riemann hypothesis. And the Claude claim comes

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:25.920
from Anthropic's own staff. Our view is that this is still the most checkable form such a claim

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:32.480
could take. No referee has to read 250k pages. The work shrinks to auditing the formal statement

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.960
and its axioms. We'd expect that to take weeks, not years. On balance, we lean toward the claim

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:45.840
while that audit is pending. For early-stage investors, this is a reference case. Startups

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.240
selling auditable AI reasoning, proof-checking or statement auditing, now have a very large

00:02:52.240 --> 00:03:00.660
example of why buyers might need them. The second story stays with AI doing hard work,

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.760
this time in software. Earlier this week OpenAI published an internal analysis of its coding

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:13.240
agents which chandra kumar r pili shared it tracks the zero intervention success rate meaning the

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:20.440
agent finishes with no human stepping in the tasks are sized at two to four human hours in march that

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:28.680
rate was 23 percent by october it was 46 percent open ai itself attributes part of the rise to

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:35.080
changes in which tasks people chose to delegate raymez nam reads open ai's figures differently

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:42.780
He points to the company's internal horizon at 80% success and says it is growing more slowly than forecasts based on meters work.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.200
Real-world task horizons, he argues, lag the predictions.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.660
On the evidence, these numbers are self-reported.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:56.120
The increase is OpenAI's own figure, and the shift in delegation may inflate it.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.600
Nam's case also relies on unverified lab data over a short window.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:08.660
Our take is that this is a lab's number about its own product, partly explained by users handing it different tasks.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.560
We want to see success on a fixed task set before pricing an agent business on it.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:20.040
For startups that charge per task, OpenAI's 23% to 46% doesn't transfer.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:26.240
What moves their margins is their own zero-intervention rate on tasks their customers chose.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.620
The AGI forward reading is different.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.720
A doubling in seven months on multi-hour tasks fits compounding capability.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.760
On that reading, change delegation may itself mean users trust agents with more,

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.880
and that is the adoption signal.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.160
From software agents to autonomy of a different kind.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.160
The third story is a Canadian defense deal.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.100
