00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:01.760
has secured a $50 million investment for an uncrewed combat aircraft.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.040
It is called Dominion Scout, and it is designed to fly alongside crewed Canadian fighter jets.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.120
The closest comparison is Andrell's Fury,

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:17.160
but Scout is aimed at the Royal Canadian Air Force and built on a Canadian supply chain.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.320
The partner list is the notable part.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:27.080
Pratt & Whitney Canada, part of RTX, has signed on along with Telesat, Thales Canada, and Top Aces.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:32.220
The report itself holds up. It accurately describes a commitment the company announced.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.300
Two things are still aspirations, though, an Air Force purchase and a first flight in 2029.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:44.120
There is no contract yet. Where we land is that this may help Canadian startups in autonomy,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.560
sensing, and satellite links as much as it helps Dominion.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.840
A domestic partner list this strong creates a path to a defense buyer.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.540
Without an Air Force contract, though, it is still a bet.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:05.200
For seed stage companies in autonomy, sensing and secure communications, the opportunity is a domestic integrator to sell into.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:10.180
The catch is that their revenue would depend on a contract that has not been signed.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:16.880
A fair question for any of them pitching on this program might be how much of the plan rests on that contract.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.640
The fourth and last of the main stories goes back to model design.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.180
Today thinking machines released an open-weight research model, a mixture of experts.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:35.680
The twist is that the middle 12 of 24 layers can loop at inference, running 1 to 5 times.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.260
Each extra pass adds compute.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:46.060
Active parameters rise from 3.4 billion with one pass to 7.4 billion at five passes.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:52.180
The headline claim is that the score on SWE Bench Verified, the coding benchmark,

00:06:52.180 --> 00:07:01.380
rises from 56.3% to 79.6%. How well does that hold? The picture is mixed. The release documents

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:07.900
the looping design. The SWE Bench Jump, however, is not in the model card we fetched. For now,

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:14.080
the headline result is a claim we could not check. What we can confirm is a 24-layer model

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:20.800
trained to rerun its middle layers up to 5 times. We think adaptive depth like this is promising

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:27.280
for self-hosted inference, but it is unproven here. The practical angle is cost. A startup

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.180
self-hosting an open model could tune its spend on each query by choosing the loop count.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:40.780
That only works if quality holds, and the extra depth is not free. Going from 3.4 billion to 7.4

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:47.780
billion active parameters roughly doubles compute per token. Before building a cost model on this,

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:52.000
We'd want to see an independent benchmark run at each loop count.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.080
